00:00.560 --> 00:13.600
Okay, this should be simple. Thankfully, it was already asked who is using coding agents, and most of you do, so that's good for me.

00:14.340 --> 00:20.840
I'm going to talk about AI coding agents in an economics team. My name is Alex.

00:22.540 --> 00:28.560
I'm the engineering lead of a cross-functional team called Economic Decision Science at Amazon. We're based in London.

00:28.560 --> 00:44.300
Our target is optimizing how the European stores operate, in any form or shape we can, including convincing others to act on our decisions and our economic models.

00:45.740 --> 01:01.560
This team is quite new. We didn't have an established place in Europe; there were central teams in the US. Initially, it was all of us. Then the team had a lot of problems: we started seeing bottlenecks in terms of data and scaling.

01:02.040 --> 01:25.140
When we engineers came in, one of the goals I set was: the scalable workforce here is not tech, it's not engineering. It's making sure that if we hire 40 more economists, we're not blocked, and we don't have to scale engineers at the same ratio.

01:26.520 --> 01:39.260
So, can economists deliver without engineering bottlenecks of all shapes and forms? That led us to certain decisions, like focusing on minimal infrastructure and fast iteration.

01:40.320 --> 02:01.080
And a hard thing to convince people of: more often than not, there's no trade-off between exploration and production. You can iterate super fast, the way you normally would, without a stage where you hand things off to people who scale it in six months. It goes straight to production, if things are set up in the right way.

02:01.420 --> 02:21.900
What does set up in the right way mean? We call it the factory of economics: these are the capabilities you should have, the things you should be able to do. And we create building blocks you can mix and match, that are very easy to use. Good UX.

02:23.220 --> 02:38.720
So you can tackle all these ambiguous problems. We don't work in one domain, we work across domains, and we have to move faster than the parts of the company that have an established location, an established charter, and can afford multi-year efforts.

02:40.340 --> 02:52.980
Before going to coding agents, I'm going to show the same chart shown in an earlier talk. There's an important point: we had all these things before AI.

02:54.600 --> 03:10.900
When we talk about capabilities, those of you who are into this will recognize them as agent skills. It ties in really well: the ways of working we had converged really well with the ways of working AI has.

03:11.240 --> 03:21.040
So we started adopting coding agents as soon as they were available to us, around the middle of last year.

03:22.180 --> 03:34.040
People started adopting them. I was doing the advocacy, never mandating, just getting people to see what they could do.

03:34.260 --> 03:58.680
But when the Opus family of models came in, Opus 4.6, there was a difference in quality. It should be relatable to many of you: you have to do less babysitting, they're better at tool calling, they're better at long tasks and combining things, and, in some situations, at one-shotting things.

03:59.900 --> 04:21.540
The interesting thing is that these agents work where we work, where the economists work. We have remote computers, and the command line was already the thing we were training people on. So the transition to this interface [unclear].

04:22.940 --> 04:37.580
So here come the insights. What do our economists, who are meant to be builders, work on? Roughly, these are the buckets, for the cross-functional team as a whole.

04:38.220 --> 04:49.620
One is the core economic work: analysis and research design, where you're rubber ducking a lot. We said they're builders, so a lot of it is data manipulation: data pipelines.

04:50.180 --> 04:56.340
There's troubleshooting: libraries are broken, things aren't working. The agent helps you there.

04:56.700 --> 05:11.280
Operations, which ideally shouldn't be there; we try to be zero ops. But sometimes there are things to keep the live systems running, because we deliver for production, and either we engineers or the economists manage those live systems.

05:11.880 --> 05:15.920
And the one that might seem obvious: writing and comms.

05:19.420 --> 05:29.640
I'll go through three examples really fast. These are anonymized, so they might seem generic, but they're real, very representative, and very valuable.

05:30.160 --> 05:47.900
A typical one: you have some science that's applied in some form. It could be an economic model; let's simplify and say it's SQL. Someone says: hey, explain this SQL to me. It doesn't matter how long it is. The agent explains it to you, step by step.

05:48.140 --> 06:02.400
You use your judgment and you spot something wrong, like: it's counting the wrong window, there's a problem here. And you conversationally get it to correct it.

06:02.600 --> 06:13.420
If you're set up in the right way, that can be in production immediately. While seemingly simple, it's very powerful, and you see a lot of it.

06:16.320 --> 06:32.840
Then you have the more exploratory work. You're starting to explore a specific problem, you have a lot of instincts, and you're basically rubber ducking with the agent: explaining things to externalize your own thoughts.

06:33.560 --> 06:46.920
And you're thinking: what about this type of methodology? What about these dimensions? Power analysis, clustering. What are my assumptions? And you start creating combinations of these methodologies.

06:46.980 --> 06:55.980
The nice thing is you're not just chatting and getting some insights. As you go along, you're actually proving and validating a lot of these things.

06:56.420 --> 07:06.620
Throughout that experience you're producing applied science that immediately gets you where you want to be. In this real case,

07:07.520 --> 07:16.200
fifteen different methodologies, or the same methodology with different variations, until you get to something you really trust and can move forward with.

07:16.860 --> 07:31.640
Then we have the writing. As economists, you know it doesn't really matter if your science is right if you don't get to have an effect on the business in some form.

07:32.160 --> 07:53.560
You may relate to being in meetings where your methodology gets challenged by non-economists. It's very cheap to challenge it, and in the past it might have been very hard for you to produce the right data, come back, and win that battle. For the company, of course.

07:54.120 --> 08:07.620
Now, maybe you don't open your laptop and prove it right away. But two minutes after, you can open it, and if you're set up the right way, you pull the data, you produce a chart, and you draft an appropriate response.

08:08.860 --> 08:20.180
I'll briefly acknowledge that we also have AI and ML in more traditional approaches, where we model behavior.

08:23.560 --> 08:36.380
But coding agents live where you live, and have the broadest space to work in, which really allows you to delegate everything you want to, if you feel safe enough.

08:36.460 --> 08:48.000
Now we get to an important part. We talked about what we delegate, and we see this across our team, because our team in its entirety uses this.

08:48.860 --> 09:07.000
What do experts in the matter not delegate? They don't delegate which methodology to select. It's very common that very eager, helpful engineers from non-embedded teams come in and say: hey, I created this awesome AI tool.

09:08.020 --> 09:19.280
You define a problem, and it picks some methodology and does it all for you. And immediately the comments are: why this methodology? It's wrong. Everything is wrong.

09:19.640 --> 09:23.720
Because they didn't have a customer in mind [unclear].

09:23.840 --> 09:37.220
So you're not delegating [unclear], or economic interpretation. You may use code to help you evaluate some things, but at the end, you apply your own judgment.

09:38.160 --> 09:41.460
Again, institutional knowledge and stakeholder management: you know how the company works.

09:41.860 --> 09:49.240
And, because I love a catch-all variable, taste: knowing what questions matter, and what you want to learn.

09:50.480 --> 09:57.420
So this is the good and the bad of our usage. Where are we finding pain points?

