11:47.620 --> 12:00.940
Here we come to the research assistant analogy. Analogies have limitations, so don't stretch this one too much, but I've seen other economists start to apply it as well, when thinking about fully autonomous agents:

12:02.000 --> 12:10.100
leaving them to do their thing, and [unclear] a human.

12:10.580 --> 12:20.460
So the scenario I want you to think about is: I hire a coding agent, as if it were a human. I hire them, and we work on a problem together.

12:20.880 --> 12:37.560
Our problem has to do with an economic model. There's a financial number, like a 100k budget, cost, whatever. There's an ask, and there's a scope I target. Maybe you're targeting Europe.

12:38.720 --> 12:43.780
So me and the agent work like you do nowadays: we pair program, you hold its hand a bit.

12:44.360 --> 12:54.500
And since you already know a few good practices, you'll separate things. I'll codify things in a deterministic layer, so I'll have a Python script at the end of it.

12:55.060 --> 12:59.340
I'll also have a data gathering and transformation layer.

