13:47.420 --> 14:05.340
Things you can encode. And, jumping to the end, you've already encoded a qualification exam. That means you can replace the agent, or any part of the agent, and your user story persists.

14:05.500 --> 14:07.260
So you have a sort of qualification gate.

14:11.680 --> 14:29.040
To make it more concrete, because people are like, I don't understand what you're talking about: it could look like this. It doesn't have to, but this is a format called YAML. It's human-readable, computer-readable, and very easy to generate.

14:29.680 --> 14:51.460
If you followed the earlier talk about long-running tasks: as you have non-deterministic steps, the errors compound and you lose control. But if you bound the non-deterministic aspects, you can cut off that propagation.

14:52.060 --> 15:13.460
One of the ways is this: I cut off the propagation at the library level. I make my things very easy for the LLM to call. So you can ask: for this natural-language request, can you map it to the right code? It does. Once you get there, you're very safe in at least one aspect.

15:14.160 --> 15:32.700
Then you could say: I know what this is. It looks like an integration test, or an eval. What about LLM-as-a-judge, where instead of determining correctness deterministically, another foundation model does it for me?

15:34.160 --> 15:42.760
You should almost resist the urge to go there, because there's cost and other considerations. But you could use it for something like testing reasoning, at least at this stage.

15:42.760 --> 16:04.160
In this case, it illustrates some of these aspects. It has a more complex ask, and what I'm doing there is trying to fail fast, to avoid going down tangents of work that have no value for me.

16:06.460 --> 16:10.100
Some of you might recognize these as feedback loops.

16:14.860 --> 16:46.440
Once you've codified the example, when things fail, you or the agent can modify some aspect, the prompt, the tools, or other things, until you get to the business use case you want. This idea of feedback loops is very important for letting things self-validate, [unclear], even self-mutate, and you control to which degree. A lot of it is instinct and practice: knowing how much leverage you want to give it.

16:47.380 --> 17:02.980
Solving the right problem is usually about fast iteration. Look up Paul MacCready's story: he did things way faster than others.

17:03.560 --> 17:22.520
But in terms of the operational realities we have, it's not all beautiful. If you're not set up with fast tools, tools that allow fast iteration even without AI, you're not going to see the benefits, and you're going to say it doesn't work. And context management is still a challenge.

17:23.780 --> 17:38.280
None of what I've said is security. Security is a different space. Security means it must not happen, so you should have deterministic policies that prevent what shouldn't happen. That's a very important thing to note.

17:38.280 --> 17:52.520
And you should be a good boss. A good boss can delegate, but owns the outcomes. You have no one to blame when you're working with agents. If you really automate, you don't have an employee you can fire.

17:54.180 --> 18:16.940
As takeaways, to improve your own work with coding agents, or the work in your team: sketch the workflow early, when you start designing things. Think about the diagram, try the UX, and see how it works. You can slowly fill the fake workflow with real things as you create the exam.

18:17.500 --> 18:39.560
Break long tasks into testable blocks, and let the agents help you there, coding prototypes that then become the real thing. Use code for the stable steps, and let the LLMs do reasoning or tool calling, but not more. Reduce the number of smart parts.

18:39.940 --> 19:01.300
So overall, with exam-first iteration, you can keep control. That works for what you're building on your own computer, and it can also be easily handed to engineers and other teams. My hope is that it can be an easy communication bridge between business, software and economics.

19:01.800 --> 19:03.940
Any questions?
